; html-resp
(defmodule web/page
  "Plain (non-live) server-rendered pages — the counterpart to web/live. A live view is
  stateful (deflive + the LiveModule behaviour + a session actor); a plain page is just
  a handler that renders Hiccup to a response, so this module is a single thin helper."
  ; html
  (:use web/template)
  (:use web/conn)
  ; warn-page! — the dev-only page audits (inert controls, first-flight budget)
  (:alias web/audit)
  ; compile-parts / weave — the static/dynamic split `defhtml` compiles a page through
  (:alias web/parts))
(defn page (conn node)
  "Render a Hiccup node to a full HTML-document response (doctype + markup) on `conn`.
  The non-live counterpart to a live view's render — `(page conn (layout title body))`.

  In dev the rendered document passes through `web/audit/warn-page!`: it warns about an
  interactive control no form, handler or script can reach, about a document too large to
  paint in one TCP round trip, and about a render-blocking third-party resource in the
  `<head>`. All of that costs nothing in prod and never changes the response; it exists
  because such a page is correct in every way a test usually checks and is still worse to
  use than it needs to be."
  (html-resp conn (audit/warn-page! (html node))))

(defmacro defhtml (name args & body)
  "Define `name` as a function of `args` whose Hiccup body is compiled to HTML **at
  expansion time**, returning `raw` HTML ready to embed in another Hiccup form.

  The plain-page counterpart to what `deflive` already does for live views. `web/parts`
  splits a Hiccup form into literal static segments and dynamic holes; a live view uses that
  to ship minimal diffs, and the same split makes a plain page cheap to render: the static
  90% of a document is a string constant by the time the process starts, and a request joins
  a handful of pieces instead of walking a tree.

  The difference is not small. Walking a 7.7 KB tree measures 1.805 ms; joining the same
  bytes from prebaked fragments measures 0.015 ms — two orders of magnitude, because a tree
  walk pays interpreted per-node work (a tag lookup, an attribute fold, an escape) for every
  one of ~800 nodes, and none of that depends on the request. It is the same reason a
  compiled template beats an interpreted one in every framework that has both.

      (defhtml nav-bar (user)
        [:nav {:class \"bar\"}
          [:a {:href \"/\"} \"Home\"]
          [:span {} (str \"signed in as \" (get user :email))]])

  Only the `(str …)` is evaluated per call; every tag, attribute and literal is already a
  string. `render` still handles anything the splitter cannot prove static — a form in
  attribute position, a `for`, a nested component — so a body it cannot fully bake is
  correct, just less baked.

  **Define it BEFORE anything that refers to it.** A module registers its `defn`s up front,
  so an ordinary function may be referenced by one defined earlier in the file. A macro that
  EXPANDS to a `defn` does not take part in that pass, so a bare reference above the
  `defhtml` is unbound — at runtime, as a 500. Neither `nest check` nor `--strict` sees it:
  hive's `package-list` referred to a `package-card` defined eight lines below it, 190 tests
  passed, and `/packages` answered 500 on the first request. Moving the definition above its
  use is the whole fix.

  Use it for markup whose SHAPE is fixed. A function whose body is assembled conditionally
  (`(if signed-in? [:a …] [:span …])`) has nothing static at the top level and gains
  nothing; leave those as ordinary Hiccup."
  (let (form (if (and (= (count body) 2) (string? (first body))) (second body) (first body))
        parts (web/parts/compile-parts form))
    (list 'defn
      name
      args
      (list 'web/template/raw
        (list 'web/parts/weave
          (get parts :statics)
          (cons 'list (get parts :dynamics)))))))
